{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"DOI:\"10.1073/pnas.1910837117\"","resultType":"core","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":25,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"31932426","source":"MED","pmid":"31932426","pmcid":"PMC6994979","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC6994979"]},"doi":"10.1073/pnas.1910837117","title":"A scalable pipeline for designing reconfigurable organisms.","authorString":"Kriegman S, Blackiston D, Levin M, Bongard J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Kriegman S","lastName":"Kriegman","firstName":"Sam","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9207-1775"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science, University of Vermont, Burlington, VT 05405."}]}},{"fullName":"Blackiston D","lastName":"Blackiston","firstName":"Douglas","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biology, Tufts University, Medford, MA 02153."},{"affiliation":"Allen Discovery Center, Tufts University, Medford, MA 02153."}]}},{"fullName":"Levin M","lastName":"Levin","firstName":"Michael","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biology, Tufts University, Medford, MA 02153."},{"affiliation":"Allen Discovery Center, Tufts University, Medford, MA 02153."},{"affiliation":"Wyss Institute for Biologically Inspired Engineering, Harvard University, Boston, MA 02115."}]}},{"fullName":"Bongard J","lastName":"Bongard","firstName":"Josh","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science, University of Vermont, Burlington, VT 05405; josh.bongard@uvm.edu."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9207-1775"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","recommended_reviews","wikipedia","related_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"117","journalIssueId":2905708,"dateOfPublication":"2020 Jan","monthOfPublication":1,"yearOfPublication":2020,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A","title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","essn":"1091-6490","issn":"0027-8424","nlmid":"7505876","isoabbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A"},"printPublicationDate":"2020-01-01"},"pubYear":"2020","pageInfo":"1853-1859","abstractText":"Living systems are more robust, diverse, complex, and supportive of human life than any technology yet created. However, our ability to create novel lifeforms is currently limited to varying existing organisms or bioengineering organoids in vitro. Here we show a scalable pipeline for creating functional novel lifeforms: AI methods automatically design diverse candidate lifeforms in silico to perform some desired function, and transferable designs are then created using a cell-based construction toolkit to realize living systems with the predicted behaviors. Although some steps in this pipeline still require manual intervention, complete automation in future would pave the way to designing and deploying unique, bespoke living systems for a wide range of functions.","affiliation":"Department of Computer Science, University of Vermont, Burlington, VT 05405.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Research Support, U.S. Gov't, Non-P.H.S.","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"DOD | Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency","grantId":"HR0011-18-2-0022","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Science Foundation","grantId":"CBET-0939511","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Paul G. Allen Family Foundation","grantId":"N/A","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Embryo, Nonmammalian","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CY","qualifierName":"cytology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y